from data_ma import W
BATCH={'key':'vtn_gflc','label':'Orleans County, Vermont','added':'2026-11-19 12:30'}
VHS=['Vermont Historical Society: Essex and Orleans county museum directory','https://vermonthistory.org/essex-and-orleans-county-museum-directory']
def HM(n,t): return ['HMdb: '+t,'https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=%d'%n]
PLACES=[
N('old-stone-house-brownington','Old Stone House (Athenian Hall)','Brownington','vtn','civic','standing',44.83602,-72.16568,'site','open','drive','','1834–1836',
  'Alexander Twilight, the first African-American college graduate in the country, was principal of the Orleans County Grammar School and minister of the Brownington church. When his trustees would not build a dormitory he built one himself: four storeys of granite, begun in 1834 and finished in 1836, which he called Athenian Hall. It housed boarding pupils until 1859, two years after his death. It stood empty until it was auctioned in 1918, when a railroad man bid for its stone to use in bridge abutments; the Orleans County Historical Society won it for $500 (the society\'s own directory entry gives 1916) and opened it as a museum in 1925.',
  'A four-storey granite block, rare in a county where nobody built in granite; where the stone came from is not known. Inside are 22 or 23 rooms of exhibits (the sources differ), with furniture, paintings, folk art, tools and "town rooms" for eleven of the county\'s towns. Across the road is Twilight\'s own house of about 1830, now the visitor centre; the museum village also has the Cyrus Eaton house (about 1834), the Samuel Read Hall house (1831), the Lawrence barn (moved here in 1997) and 55 acres of farmland.',
  'The granite school dormitory the country\'s first Black college graduate built with his own hands.',
  'Owned and run by the Orleans County Historical Society at 109 Old Stone House Road. The Vermont Historical Society\'s directory gives May 15 to October 15, Wednesday to Sunday, 11 am to 4 pm, adults $10 (Brownington residents free). The museum\'s own visit page refused the tool (HTTP 403), so those hours are not checked against it. Parking at the museum (not checked on the ground).',
  [W('Old Stone House Museum'),W('Brownington Village Historic District'),HM(85871,'Old Stone House'),HM(221756,'Alexander Twilight and Natural History'),VHS,['Old Stone House Museum','https://oldstonehousemuseum.org/']],brick='no'),
N('brownington-congregational-church','Brownington Congregational Church','Brownington','vtn','worship','standing',44.83675,-72.16858,'exact','view','drive','','1841–',
  'For eighteen years the congregation met upstairs in the Orleans County Grammar School; its church was finished in 1841. Alexander Twilight and the teacher Samuel Read Hall both preached here. In 1899 William Barstow Strong, a Brownington boy who went to the grammar school and became president of the Atchison, Topeka and Santa Fe Railroad, paid to remodel the interior and gave the bell and spire.',
  'The church and its village graveyard on Hinman Settler Road. At the front of the graveyard, near the road and facing the Old Stone House, are the graves of Alexander and Mercy Twilight. Interior not checked.',
  'Where Alexander Twilight preached, and where he is buried.',
  'Part of the Brownington Village historic district at 1485 Hinman Settler Road. The graveyard is beside the road; whether and when the church is open was not found, so treat the building as seen from outside. Park at the Old Stone House Museum, a short walk east (not checked on the ground).',
  [HM(221726,'Brownington Congregational Church and Cemetery'),W('Brownington Village Historic District')],brick='?'),
N('orleans-county-grammar-school','Orleans County Grammar School and Prospect Hill','Brownington','vtn','civic','standing',44.8374,-72.16798,'exact','view','walk','','1823–',
  'William Baxter paid for the county\'s only high school in 1823, at the foot of Prospect Hill; the church met on its upper floor until 1841, and Twilight kept an office there to watch his pupils. It closed in 1859. Ten years later its belfry came off and it was moved about a third of a mile down the road to serve as a Grange hall. In 2016 it was brought back near its first site, hauled (the papers said) by 44 oxen. Pupils climbed Prospect Hill above it to sing at evening; Strong built an observatory tower on top in the 1890s, which fell to ruin, was rebuilt for the 1975 bicentennial and again in 1998.',
  'A small two-storey school, probably red when new (traces of red paint were found), now with a porch it did not have. The observatory tower on Prospect Hill looks to the Lake Willoughby gap, Lake Memphremagog and Jay Peak, and on a clear day Mount Mansfield and the White Mountains. Interior not checked.',
  'The county\'s first high school, walked back home by oxen in 2016.',
  'Owned by the town of Brownington and part of the Old Stone House museum village, on Hinman Settler Road west of the church. The museum\'s directory entry lists the school and observatory among its buildings; how the public reaches the tower is not checked (the hill path is not on the map). Park at the museum.',
  [HM(221755,'Orleans County Grammar School and Prospect Hill Observatory'),W('Brownington Village Historic District'),VHS],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('brick-kingdom-barton','Brick Kingdom factory ruins','Barton','vtn','industry','ruins',44.74816,-72.17801,'site','public','walk','','1798–1952',
  'Willoughby Brook, the outflow of Crystal Lake, drops about 90 feet in a run of falls through Barton village. Asa Kimball built a sawmill on the upper falls in 1798 and a gristmill in 1807, and through the 19th century factories lined Water Street making chairs, carriages, woollens, machinery, piano actions, underwear, butter tubs and furniture; at their height they employed about 300. Fires and closings took them one by one, and the last left in 1952. The district, known locally as the Brick Kingdom, was listed on the National Register in 1994.',
  'The Brick Kingdom Trail, a short path across the falls behind the Pierce House Museum, passes the ruins of the factories (the association\'s page says; which mill each ruin was is not checked). The Pierce House Museum shows the village\'s industry in photographs, maps and artifacts.',
  'A short trail through the factory ruins along Barton\'s falls.',
  'Kept by the Crystal Lake Falls Historical Association. The trail is open sunrise to sunset; its two entrances are by the Chronicle office and by E.M. Brown, near the museum at 97 Water Street. The Pierce House Museum is free and open Saturdays and Sundays 1 to 3 pm, June to September, or by appointment. Park along Water Street. The pin is the museum.',
  [W('Crystal Lake Falls Historic District'),['Crystal Lake Falls Historical Association','https://sites.google.com/site/bartonmuseum/home'],VHS],brick='?'),
N('crystal-lake-bathhouse-barton','Crystal Lake State Park bathhouse','Barton','vtn','inst','standing',44.74714,-72.16994,'site','public','drive','','1930s–1942',
  'The Civilian Conservation Corps laid out this lake beach in the 1930s, with its parking lot, retaining wall and most of a bathhouse designed by the New Hampshire architect David Fried. Wikipedia says local workers finished the bathhouse in 1942 after the CCC crews were disbanded for the war; the park\'s own page says the CCC built it in the late 1930s. Its granite came from the old quarry on the east shore of the lake, whose stone steamboats once carried across the water. The park was listed on the National Register in 2005.',
  'A large bathhouse of rough granite, unusual among the state\'s CCC buildings for its partly Modern design, with the CCC retaining wall and nearly a mile of sandy beach. It still holds the restrooms, changing rooms and snack stand.',
  'A CCC bathhouse of granite from across the lake, part Modern, part rustic.',
  'Vermont State Parks, 96 Bellwater Avenue off VT 16. Open the Friday of Memorial Day weekend to Labor Day weekend, 10 am to sunset; day-use fee; no pets. Outside the season the gate is not checked. Park in the park lot.',
  [W('Crystal Lake State Park'),['Vermont State Parks: Crystal Lake','https://vtstateparks.com/crystal.html']],conf='mixed',brick='no',th=[44.74636,-72.17188],thn='Crystal Lake State Park lot, Bellwater Avenue'),
N('haskell-free-library-opera-house','Haskell Free Library and Opera House','Derby Line','vtn','civic','standing',45.00574,-72.09767,'exact','open','drive','','1901–1904',
  'Martha Stewart Haskell and her son Horace gave this library and opera house to the two border villages, building it on purpose across the line between Derby Line, Vermont and Stanstead, Quebec. The opera house opened on June 7, 1904. The book stacks and the stage are in Canada and the main door and most seats in the United States, so it is called the only opera house in the country with no stage. Since 2025 American rules have limited how Canadians may reach the front door, and a new entrance on the Canadian side opened in 2026.',
  'A Queen Anne building by James Ball, with the library on the ground floor and a 500-seat theatre on the two floors above. A black line across the floors marks the border. The opera house is seen only on a guided tour with staff.',
  'A library and opera house built across the Canadian border, with the line painted on the floor.',
  'Owned by the library\'s association. American entrance at 93 Caswell Avenue, Derby Line, with a small lot behind the building; Canadian entrance at 1 Church Street, Stanstead. It is NOT a border crossing: you must leave by the door you came in, and Border Patrol or the RCMP may ask for identification. The sidewalk joining the two sides has been closed since October 1, 2025. Library hours: Tuesday to Friday 10 am to 5 pm, Saturday 10 am to 4 pm, closed Sunday and Monday. Opera house tours $10 an adult, under 12 free, 30 to 45 minutes; booking recommended. No large bags, food or pets.',
  [W('Haskell Free Library and Opera House'),['Haskell Free Library & Opera House: visits','https://www.haskelloperahouse.org/visits.html'],HM(75167,'Haskell Free Library and Opera House')],brick='some'),
N('greensboro-depot','Greensboro Depot','Greensboro Bend','vtn','rail','standing',44.54658,-72.26474,'exact','view','walk','','c. 1872–',
  'The Portland and Ogdensburg Railroad built this station about 1872 where its line swung through a big loop to serve Greensboro; the loop gave Greensboro Bend its name. The line later became the St. Johnsbury and Lake Champlain. The state bought it in 1973 and it is now the Lamoille Valley Rail Trail; the station buildings have been put to other uses.',
  'Two wooden buildings end-on to Main Street: the passenger station, with long eaves, Stick-style woodwork under them and jigsawn brackets in the gables, and a plainer freight house behind it. A well-kept example of the line\'s early station design. Interior not checked.',
  'An 1872 Stick-style station on the old St. Johnsbury and Lake Champlain, now beside a rail trail.',
  'At 1066 Main Street (VT 16), Greensboro Bend; the buildings are in other use and owner not checked, so see them from the street and the rail trail. The Lamoille Valley Rail Trail lot is beside the depot.',
  [W('Greensboro station (Vermont)')],brick='no',th=[44.54682,-72.26432],thn='Lamoille Valley Rail Trail lot, Greensboro Bend'),
N('orne-covered-bridge','Orne Covered Bridge','Irasburg','vtn','landmark','standing',44.86093,-72.27334,'exact','public','drive','','1879/1881–1997; replica 2000',
  'A single-span Paddleford truss over the Black River on Back Coventry Road, just south of Coventry village. Sources give 1879 or 1881 for it and E.P. or John Colton as its builder. When listed in 1974 it was the only historic Paddleford truss left on a public road in Vermont, never strengthened. An arsonist burned it in 1997; the bridge there now is a replica finished in 2000.',
  'An 87-foot one-lane bridge with vertical board siding carried round inside the portals, gables jutting three feet past the sides, a metal roof and a plank deck. All of it dates from 2000.',
  'Vermont\'s last historic Paddleford truss, burned in 1997 and built again.',
  'A town road bridge, open to traffic. Pull off on the road shoulder clear of the bridge (no lot; not checked on the ground).',
  [W('Orne Covered Bridge')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('orleans-county-courthouse','Orleans County Courthouse','Newport','vtn','civic','standing',44.93507,-72.21072,'exact','view','drive','','1886–',
  'In 1886 the legislature moved the county seat from isolated Irasburg to Newport, then a busy lake and railroad town, and the county built this Romanesque courthouse, designed by the local architect Lambert Packard. The complex once also had an 1886 wooden jailer\'s house and a 1903 brick jail, a mail-order design from the Pauly Jail Company of St. Louis; both were razed in 2022.',
  'A brick courthouse with a hip roof, a projecting centre section rising into a two-stage clock tower, round-arched doorways and windows, blind arches and bands of corbelled brick. Interior not checked; it is a working court.',
  'The courthouse that took the county seat from Irasburg in 1886.',
  'Orleans County court building on the south side of Main Street between Second and Third streets, downtown Newport. Seen from the street; entering a working courthouse means security screening (hours not checked). Street parking downtown.',
  [W('Orleans County Courthouse and Jail Complex')],brick='yes'),
N('newport-federal-building-vt','U.S. Courthouse, Post Office and Customs House','Newport','vtn','civic','standing',44.93516,-72.21009,'exact','view','drive','','1904–',
  'Completed in 1904 to hold every federal office in this border town: the court, the post office and the customs house. The federal district court sat here until 1948. The post office lobby was rebuilt in 1930 and again after the post office left in 1967. It is the tallest building in Newport and now holds the county\'s district court.',
  'Four storeys: a ground floor of banded grey marble and upper floors of red brick in Flemish bond, with marble keystones and sills and a dentilled cornice. Interior not checked.',
  'A 1904 border town\'s court, post office and customs house in one.',
  'At Main and Second streets, east of the county courthouse; a working court building. Seen from the street. Street parking downtown.',
  [W('United States Courthouse, Post Office and Customs House (Newport, Vermont)')],brick='yes'),
N('goodrich-memorial-library','Goodrich Memorial Library','Newport','vtn','civic','standing',44.93565,-72.21022,'exact','open','drive','','1898–1899',
  'Converse and Almira Goodrich paid for this free library; it was begun in 1898 to designs by William Storey and dedicated on September 1, 1899. When the stock that funded it failed in 1933 the town took on its keep. It was restored after a grant in 2003.',
  'A Romanesque building with fireplaces of coloured brick and rooms finished in red birch, Georgia pine, cypress, spruce and Swanton red marble, with quartered-oak furniture. Upstairs are an art room with old paintings and a floor-to-ceiling wall of glass cases of stuffed animals and curiosities (an alligator, an ostrich egg), preserved with mercury and not to be touched.',
  'An 1899 library with a cabinet of curiosities upstairs.',
  'Public library at 202 Main Street, open Monday to Friday 10 am to 6 pm and Saturday 10 am to 2 pm (its own page). Wheelchair entrance at the rear on Field Avenue. Street parking downtown.',
  [W('Goodrich Memorial Library'),['Goodrich Memorial Library','https://www.goodrichlibrary.org/']],brick='yes'),
N('irasburg-town-hall','Irasburg Town Hall','Irasburg','vtn','civic','standing',44.80255,-72.27812,'exact','view','drive','','1911–',
  'Irasburg was the shire town of Orleans County until the seat went to Newport in the 1880s. Its old courthouse on the green burned in 1910, and the town built this hall on the site in 1911. Besides town business it has housed travelling and local plays, dances and dinners; that use fell off in the 1970s and has since come back.',
  'A wooden hall like a big Foursquare house, with a hipped roof, dormers and a columned front porch. Upstairs is an auditorium with a vaulted ceiling of arched panels and five painted backdrops of northern Vermont scenes by local artists. Interior not checked in person.',
  'A town hall on the old courthouse site, with painted stage scenery upstairs.',
  'Town building on the east side of Irasburg Square (VT 14/58), between the library and the store. Seen from the green; whether the hall is open outside events is not checked.',
  [W('Irasburg Town Hall')],brick='no'),
N('holland-congregational-church-vt','Holland Congregational Church','Holland','vtn','worship','standing',44.96952,-72.03681,'exact','ask','drive','','1844–1854',
  'Holland\'s only surviving 19th-century public building. Wikipedia, from the Register form, says the frame went up in 1844 and the church was finished ten years later; the Vermont Historical Society\'s directory says it was built in 1848. The congregation lasted until 1954, and in 1973 a historical society formed to save it took it over; it is now the society\'s museum and meeting house.',
  'A Greek Revival meeting house in open country with a three-stage tower and spire. Inside: the original carved pulpit and minister\'s chair, a band of tulip stencilling round the tops of the walls, a gallery up a narrow winding stair and a kitchen of about 1880. The museum holds photographs, furnishings, textiles, farm tools and town memorabilia.',
  'An 1844 country church kept with its stencilled walls and carved pulpit.',
  'Holland Historical Society, 693 Gore Road. Open June to September by appointment, free (its directory entry, which gives the phone number); Old Home Day is the first Sunday in August. Park at the church (not checked on the ground).',
  [W('Holland Congregational Church'),['Town of Holland: Holland Historical Society','https://hollandvt.gov/holland-historical-society/'],VHS],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('sweeney-store-east-charleston','J. S. Sweeney Store','East Charleston','vtn','civic','standing',44.83771,-71.98982,'exact','view','drive','','c. 1880–',
  'East Charleston grew up as a lumber village on the Clyde River from 1820. A fire in 1921 took nearly all of it; only this store and the church across the road came through. The store dates from about 1880. A barn of about 1860 and a livery with a hall upstairs, a stagecoach stop, also survived the fire and were listed with the store in 1992, but were pulled down after that.',
  'A two-storey wooden general store with the keeper\'s rooms above, a two-storey recessed porch on paneled posts, and plain Greek Revival trim; a typical northern Vermont store. Interior not checked.',
  'The store that outlasted the fire that took its village in 1921.',
  'On the south side of VT 105 in East Charleston, opposite the Congregational church. Shop below and dwellings above; whether it still trades is not checked. Seen from the road.',
  [W('J. S. Sweeney Store, Barn, Livery and Hall')],brick='no'),
N('runaway-pond-glover','Runaway Pond','Glover','vtn','water','marker',44.63267,-72.21132,'area','view','drive','','1810',
  'Long Pond, about a mile and a half long, emptied in one day. On June 6, 1810, in a dry summer, about sixty men and boys dug a new outlet north to feed the Barton River for Aaron Willson\'s gristmill. The bank gave way, the whole pond ran out in about an hour and a quarter, and the flood tore down the valley to Lake Memphremagog in four hours, raising it about a foot. Nobody died. The empty bed has been called Runaway Pond since.',
  'A state roadside marker (2010) on the site; the old pond bed is now a marsh. Wikipedia says the flood\'s work can still be seen at Clark Pond just north and along the Barton River valley (not checked on the ground).',
  'The pond that ran away in 1810.',
  'The marker stands beside VT 16 about five miles south of Glover village, on HMdb\'s position. The marsh around it is not shown as public land; see it from the road shoulder. The pin is the marker.',
  [W('Runaway Pond'),HM(75120,'Runaway Pond')],brick='no'),
N('clyde-pond-dam-newport','Clyde Pond Dam and Arnolds Falls','Newport','vtn','water','standing',44.93407,-72.17669,'site','view','drive','','1918–',
  'Calvin Arnold came about 1800 and dammed the falls on the Clyde River, the first of several dams on this stretch over 150 years; a pulp mill, sawmill, gristmill, clover mill, woollen mill, tannery and nail forge followed, and the Arnolds built a brick house near the falls in 1824 that still stands. In 1890–91 Charles Prouty\'s Newport Electric Light Company built a timber-crib dam and power house below the falls, one of the first hydro plants in Vermont. The 713-foot Prouty Dam, now the Clyde Pond Dam, followed in 1918 and is the only intact dam left on this part of the river.',
  'The 1918 dam holding Clyde Pond, seen from the marker on Crawford Farm Road; the falls drop just downstream. The 1824 Arnold house is about 100 yards from the top of the falls (which house it is not checked). OpenStreetMap calls the dam here Newport Number 11 Dam, while the federal names file lists a Newport Number 11 Dam among vanished structures about 300 m north-west; how the names fit was not settled.',
  'A 1918 power dam on falls that ran mills from 1800.',
  'Marker on the east side of Crawford Farm Road south of Clyde Street (HMdb gives 1376 Crawford Farm Road). The dam belongs to the power company (owner not checked); see it from the road. The pin is on the dam as seen on the aerial.',
  [HM(198876,'History of Hydropower on the Lower Clyde River')],conf='mixed',brick='?',th=[44.93492,-72.17603],thn='Road shoulder at the HMdb marker, Crawford Farm Road'),
N('island-pond-station','Island Pond station','Island Pond','vtn','rail','standing',44.81551,-71.88042,'exact','view','drive','','1853–1965',
  'Island Pond was the halfway point of the Grand Trunk Railway between Montreal and Portland, Maine, 149 and 147 miles away. The two halves of the line met here in July 1853, forming the first international railway in North America, and the town grew into a railroad centre with a roundhouse and shops. After the Canadian government took over the Grand Trunk in 1923 traffic went to Canadian ports; passenger service ended about 1960 and the station closed in 1965. Freight still runs on the line (the St. Lawrence and Atlantic).',
  'The station building beside the working track, now a bank branch and offices. Markers to the halfway point (1968 and later) stand nearby; another, 1,366 feet from where the two railways joined, is in the village. Building date and interior not checked.',
  'The halfway house of the Grand Trunk, where Montreal met Portland in 1853.',
  'In the village centre by the tracks of an active railroad: stay off the track. Seen from the street. The Island Pond Historical Society, which Wikipedia says has used the station, is listed at 126 Cross Street, open June to October, Saturdays and Sundays 1 to 4 pm (Vermont Historical Society directory).',
  [W('Island Pond station'),HM(75118,'Island Pond'),HM(221720,'Great Halfway Point'),HM(198518,'First International Railway in North America'),VHS],brick='?'),
]
